{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-109773",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n  \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\u27e8f(y)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={y}^{3}-{y}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=\u27e8({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y,\\,({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9,\\,y\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta .$",
